<component><section><templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.17" extension="2015-08-01"/><code code="29762-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Social history Narrative"/><title>Social History</title><text><list><item>Born in West Africa; taken from her family by traffickers as a young child and brought to Boston, where she has lived since in the household of the family who acquired her. She works in the house as a domestic companion without wages, does not hold her own identity papers, and the household decides where she may travel. She knows nothing of her birth family [S]</item><item>Taught to read and write in the household; reads the Bible, Latin and Greek classics and English poetry; a published poet whose first book is being prepared for publication abroad. Writes in the early morning and late at night. Her sources of strength are her faith, her writing and her correspondence with a close friend [S]</item><item>Exposures: no tobacco, vaping, alcohol or drug use; cooks over a gas range without a vented hood and tends the wood fireplaces in the house; walks the steep streets of the old town in winter wind [S]</item><item>Not sexually active; has not been pregnant; no one pressures her about sex or pregnancy (asked privately) [S]</item><item>Seen alone for part of the visit, as every patient is; she is an adult and makes her own decisions. Asked privately, she says she is not physically harmed, and that she hopes to win her freedom in her own way; she accepted written information and the clinic social worker&apos;s direct line (555-0142), and declined other help for now. Results and plans go to her alone, and the clinic takes no instructions about her care from the household [S]</item></list></text></section></component>

